Model Card for the BBO Optimisation Approach

1. Overview

Model Name: Adaptive Heuristic BBO Strategy
Type: Sequential, query-limited black-box optimisation approach
Version: v1.0 (Ten-round capstone implementation)
This is not a machine learning model, but a structured optimisation decision framework applied across eight unknown functions.

2. Intended Use

Suitable for:
•	Low-budget black-box optimisation problems
•	Educational demonstrations of adaptive search
•	Small-dimensional search spaces
•	Scenarios where interpretability of decisions matters
Should be avoided for:
•	High-dimensional optimisation
•	Noisy or adversarial objective functions
•	Time-critical real-world deployment
•	Guaranteed global optimisation requirements

3. Details of the Strategy

Phase 1: Exploration (Rounds 1–3)
•	Sampled diverse regions
•	Identified rough function curvature
•	Observed sensitivity of parameters
Phase 2: Structured Probing (Rounds 4–6)
•	Focused on promising areas
•	Adjusted step sizes
•	Tested local variability
Phase 3: Exploitation (Rounds 7–9)
•	Narrowed search region
•	Reduced step magnitude
•	Prioritized incremental improvement
Phase 4: Local Refinement (Round 10)
•	Fine-grained adjustments
•	Limited re-exploration to avoid premature convergence
The approach was heuristic, adaptive, and path-dependent.

4. Performance

Evaluation metric:
•	Best observed objective value per function
•	Improvement trajectory across rounds
•	Stability of improvements
Summary across eight functions:
•	Consistent improvement in early rounds
•	Diminishing returns after round 7
•	Likely convergence to local optima in some cases
•	No guarantee of global optimality
Because true optima were unknown, performance was measured relatively, not absolutely.

5. Assumptions and Limitations

Key Assumptions:
1.	Local smoothness of objective functions
2.	Informative feedback from each query
3.	Stability of patterns across nearby points
4.	Limited noise
Limitations:
•	Only ten queries per function
•	No probabilistic uncertainty modelling
•	No formal convergence guarantees
•	Susceptibility to local optima
•	Manual heuristic adjustments
Failure Modes:
•	Highly discontinuous functions
•	Deceptive landscapes
•	High-dimensional search spaces

6. Ethical Considerations

While this is a technical optimisation project, transparency matters for:
•	Reproducibility
•	Fair evaluation of performance
•	Avoiding overstated claims
•	Clear articulation of assumptions
Documenting strategy phases and decision logic ensures that results are not presented as purely outcome-driven but as shaped by bounded rational decision-making.
Transparency also supports adaptation: others can modify the strategy, critique assumptions, or test improvements.

Reflection on Documentation Quality

Strengths of this Model Card:
•	Clear breakdown of phases
•	Explicit assumptions
•	Honest discussion of limitations
•	Defined intended use boundaries
Potential Improvements:
•	Quantitative convergence graphs
•	Comparative baseline (e.g. random search)
•	Formal pseudocode
•	Sensitivity analysis summary
However, given the project scale and educational purpose, the current structure is sufficient to:
•	Communicate decision logic
•	Support reproducibility
•	Make limitations explicit
•	Avoid overclaiming generalisability

Final Reflection

Together, the datasheet and model card:
•	Separate data documentation from strategy documentation
•	Make decision processes visible
•	Clarify assumptions and trade-offs
•	Enhance transparency and reproducibility

In optimisation as in machine learning performance numbers alone are insufficient. 
Clear documentation of data generation, decision logic, assumptions, and constraints are essential for responsible evaluation and meaningful comparison.
